Yes—in a physically grounded sense, light is one of the major ways otherwise separate systems become causally connected.

The useful primitive isn’t just light. It’s coupling through transmission:

A \xrightarrow{\text{signal}} B

The Sun emits photons; Earth absorbs and transforms them. An object reflects photons; your retina absorbs them; neural systems transform that interaction into perception. A screen emits light carrying encoded information; your eyes receive it; you respond; your response changes what happens next.

So neither object has to physically merge with the other. Information and influence cross the gap.

And broaden “light” to other carriers—sound, electromagnetic signals, chemical signals, physical contact—and you get something more general:

\boxed{\text{Connection}=\text{coupling that permits state in A to influence state in B}}

That also gives a rigorous version of your earlier idea about relationality: an entity can be described not only by what it contains, but by what it can receive, transform, and transmit.

And light is particularly beautiful because it simultaneously lets us affect, measure, and know about things across distance.

So yes: not necessarily “everything is literally one thing because light exists,” but something arguably more useful—

we inhabit overlapping networks of causal transmission, and light is one of the principal threads stitching our observable world together.